/// Cached resolution of a provider place into the identities it searches by.
///
/// A cache, never a source of truth: truncating it costs one extra provider
/// call per place and changes no behaviour. It exists because resolving a
/// selected place is a billed request, and a public search page would otherwise
/// pay it on every visit.
///
/// Identity keys are cached rather than the SearchArea rows they matched. That
/// distinction matters: a place with no competitions yet caches successfully
/// with keys that match nothing, and starts returning results the moment a
/// competition is ingested there — with no invalidation step. Caching matched
/// ids instead would freeze that emptiness in place.
/// Why a cached resolution exists.
///
/// The two states are not degrees of the same thing. RESOLVED with no identity
/// keys means "this place is real and nothing is stored under it" — a correct
/// answer that starts returning results once something is ingested there.
/// NOT_FOUND means the provider refused the id outright, and is cached only so
/// a permanently dead id stops costing a billed lookup on every request.
///
/// Transient failures are deliberately absent: caching one would freeze an
/// outage into a permanent wrong answer.
enum PlaceResolutionStatus {
  RESOLVED
  NOT_FOUND
}

